    <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeLarge28">28</sys:Double>
    <!-- The theme now owns its display sizes (HYP-211). These were consumed in four places
         but defined nowhere in source/ - they resolved only because Playnite's default theme
         defines them and merges first, which meant the Details View game title was sized by
         a number this theme never declared, and a Playnite release changing it would have
         silently resized the flagship surface.

         Values deliberately match Playnite's current 20/29 rather than the 20/28 the
         abandoned foundations branch used. Owning the value is the point; changing it is a
         separate decision, and 28 would have shrunk the game title by 1px for no stated
         reason. It would also not have stopped there: FontSizeLarger is read by four
         installed plugin DLLs (checkdlc, gameactivity, howlongtobeat,
         ScreenshotUtilitiesLocalProvider) and FontSizeLargest by gameactivity, so these two
         keys resize other people's UI as well as ours. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeLarger">20</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeLargest">29</sys:Double>

    <!-- Hero numeral, Nightglass (HYP-249). One surface only: the achievement-completion
         percentage on the Details trophy showcase. Deliberately NOT applied to the game
         titles - the Grid title is pinned to FontSizeLarge22 to avoid wrapping in the 300px
         column, and 52 would make that worse. No consumers wired in this issue. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="FontSizeDisplay">52</sys:Double>

    <!-- Weight tokens. Named for the role, not the number, so a weight change is one edit.
         Deliberately NOT called FontDisplay/FontBody/FontLabel as the issue proposed: WPF
         keeps family and weight on separate properties, so a single resource cannot carry
         "Inter Tight SemiBold", and a key named FontDisplay holding a bare FontWeight would
         lie about its own type. Pair these with TightFont / FontFamily above. -->
    <FontWeight x:Key="FontWeightDisplay">SemiBold</FontWeight>
    <FontWeight x:Key="FontWeightBody">Regular</FontWeight>
    <FontWeight x:Key="FontWeightLabel">Medium</FontWeight>
    <!-- The theme ships its own fonts in Typefaces/, but reaching them is awkward.

         The directory is NOT called Fonts/ on purpose. Toolbox's packer blacklists
         "^Fonts\\" (Playnite.Toolbox/Themes.cs, PackageFileBlackListRegex) because its
         scaffolder copies Playnite's own common fonts there, so anything a theme puts in
         Fonts/ is silently dropped from the .pthm - it works when deployed by hand and
         vanishes the moment the theme is packaged. Renaming the folder is the whole fix;
         the blacklist is anchored at the start of the relative path.

         Playnite parses theme XAML with Xaml.FromFile -> XamlReader.Load(stream) with no
         ParserContext, and only assigns ResourceDictionary.Source afterwards. By then the
         FontFamily objects already exist with a null BaseUri, so a theme-relative path
         ("Typefaces/#Inter") has nothing to resolve against and lands on the Playnite
         install root instead. {ThemeFile} can't rescue it either: it runs the target
         property's TypeConverter (right shape for FontFamily) but File.Exists-checks the
         relative path first, and the "#Family" fragment WPF requires makes that check fail.

         The path segment "VibeMythos_fb4d738f-..." is theme.yaml's Id - Playnite derives the
         deployed folder name from it. Change the Id and all three chains below silently
         stop resolving entry 1. CLAUDE.md carries a validator for this.

         So each entry below is a comma-separated fallback chain, most specific first:
           1. install-root-relative path to our bundled file  (portable installs under
              <PlayniteInstall>\Themes\Desktop\; misses under %AppData%\Playnite\Themes\)
           2. the bare family name                            (works if the user has it installed)
           3. a system face                                   (always resolves)
         A chain entry that fails resolution is skipped, so the worst case is exactly the
         behaviour we had before shipping any fonts - never a hard fall to Arial.

         Note step 2 is weaker than it looks: WPF family matching is fuzzy, so a bare
         "Inter Tight" or "Inter Mod" silently resolves to plain "Inter" when only Inter is
         installed. That is the bug this workstream exists to fix, and it is why a fallback
         chain alone was never sufficient - the files have to ship. It also means a bare
         "Inter" entry placed AFTER "Inter Tight"/"Inter Mod" is dead weight, and anything
         after that is unreachable on a machine with Inter present - so the chains below
         list only entries that can actually be selected.

         Do NOT bind any of these to HtmlTextView.HtmlFontFamily. That control stringifies
         FontFamily.ToString() into a *quoted* CSS font-family (Playnite/Controls/
         HtmlTextView.cs), so the whole chain would be read as one family name and match
         nothing. Use HtmlDescriptionFontFamily below. -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="FontFamily">Themes/Desktop/VibeMythos_fb4d738f-62bd-4e08-afd9-52e8cb45f6ca/Typefaces/#Inter, Inter, Segoe UI Variable Text, Segoe UI</FontFamily>
    <!-- Misnomer kept for compatibility: "Inter Mod" is a modified Inter, not a monospace,
         and its one consumer is prose (the Notes box). Terminal entries are therefore a
         prose stack, not Consolas.

         Measured 2026-08-20, because the 3.0 plan guessed it was "a modified Inter with
         tabular figures". It is not that either. Advance widths at 100px match plain Inter
         to three decimals - '0'=63.087 '1'=40.673 '7'=56.593 'i'=24.22 'M'=90.333 - so it
         is neither monospaced (i != M) nor tabular (0 != 1 != 7), and it buys nothing over
         FontFamily. Whatever "Mod" changes, it is glyph shapes, not metrics. Kept only
         because swapping it would alter those shapes on a live surface.

         For numeric UI use Typography.NumeralAlignment="Tabular" instead. Measured to work
         on all three bundled families (every digit snaps to 64.843 on Inter) and to inherit
         through ContentControl templates, so one setting on an ancestor reaches the score
         ContentControls. -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="MonospaceFontFamily">Themes/Desktop/VibeMythos_fb4d738f-62bd-4e08-afd9-52e8cb45f6ca/Typefaces/#Inter Mod, Inter Mod, Segoe UI Variable Text, Segoe UI</FontFamily>
    <!-- HtmlTextView renders through HtmlRenderer, which only sees system-installed fonts -
         the bundled files are unreachable there - and it injects this value into a quoted
         CSS declaration. It must stay a SINGLE family name: no path, no commas. -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="HtmlDescriptionFontFamily">Inter</FontFamily>
    <!-- Segoe Fluent Icons is Win11; Segoe MDL2 Assets is the Win10 fallback. Centralized
         here so the 63 glyph sites don't each carry the pair as a literal. Unlike the
         chains above this is a genuine CSS-style pair of system faces, not a path. -->
    <FontFamily x:Key="IconFontFamily">Segoe Fluent Icons, Segoe MDL2 Assets</FontFamily>

    <sys:Double x:Key="EllipseBorderThickness">1</sys:Double>
    <!-- ============================ Shape and space (HYP-198) ============================

         Five radius tiers, a 4px spacing ladder, and the two opacity values the base styles
         used to hardcode. New work uses these; the legacy radius keys below are being
         retired onto them as each surface is verified.

         RadiusPill is a sentinel, not a measurement. WPF's Border clamps CornerRadius to
         half the smaller dimension, so 999 renders a true pill or circle at whatever size
         the element happens to be - verified pixel-for-pixel: a 48x48 Border at 999 is
         byte-identical to the same Border at 24. That makes it strictly better than the
         half-height literals it replaces, which quietly stop being pills the moment
         somebody changes the control's height. ToggleButton.xaml was already in that
         state: CornerRadius="24" against a TemplateBinding Height, correct only at 48.

         Space* (Double) and Inset* (Thickness) are the same ladder in the two shapes XAML
         needs; the split is deliberate, since Margin/Padding want a Thickness and things
         like Width or a StackPanel spacing want a Double. -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusControl">6</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusInner">10</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusCard">16</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPanel">22</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="RadiusPill">999</CornerRadius>

    <!-- Off-grid 2px exception, Nightglass (HYP-249). The 4px grid is the rule; Space0 is the
         sanctioned escape for tight icon padding, matching Fluent's own rationale for its
         2/6/10 values. Use sparingly - prefer Space1 unless a site genuinely needs 2px. -->
    <sys:Double x:Key="Space0">2</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="Space1">4</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="Space2">8</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="Space3">12</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="Space4">16</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="Space5">20</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="Space6">24</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="Space7">28</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="Space8">32</sys:Double>

    <Thickness x:Key="Inset1">4</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="Inset2">8</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="Inset3">12</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="Inset4">16</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="Inset5">20</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="Inset6">24</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="Inset7">28</Thickness>
    <Thickness x:Key="Inset8">32</Thickness>

    <sys:Double x:Key="OpacityDisabled">0.4</sys:Double>
    <sys:Double x:Key="OpacitySecondary">0.6</sys:Double>
    <!-- ================================================================================== -->

    <!-- Legacy radius keys. ControlCornerRadius is Playnite-global (core chrome reads it by
         name), so it stays defined whatever happens to the rest. The others are theme-only
         and are being folded into the tiers above; what remains here are the values that do
         not sit on a tier and still need a surface-by-surface visual call. -->
    <CornerRadius x:Key="ControlCornerRadius">6</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="ControlCornerRadiusMedium">8</CornerRadius>
    <CornerRadius x:Key="SteamLinksBorderCornerRadius">21</CornerRadius>
