    <!-- Playnite's own key, typo and all. WindowBackgourndBrush is the ONLY window-background key
         Playnite core actually reads; the correctly-spelled WindowBackgroundBrush above is
         theme-owned and consumed only by this theme's MainWindowStyle. Someone fixed the spelling
         at some point and thereby stopped overriding the key that matters.

         Leaving it undefined meant it fell through to the default theme's navy gradient
         (#202b4d -> MainColorDark), which paints:
           - CustomControls/WindowBase.xaml, which this theme does not ship, so Playnite's copy
             loads and every plugin window built on it rendered navy inside a black theme;
           - eight installed plugins that reference the key by name - ThemeModifier among them,
             i.e. the window users open in order to configure this theme.

         Retinting MainColorDark alone does not fix it: the default's first GradientStop is a
         hardcoded #202b4d literal, so half the gradient would have stayed blue. Matches
         WindowBackgroundBrush and PopupBackgroundColor, both already #000000, so every window
         surface in the theme now agrees. -->
    <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="WindowBackgourndBrush" StartPoint="0.5,-0.2" EndPoint="0.5,1" >
        <GradientStop Color="#000000" Offset="0"/>
        <GradientStop Color="#000000" Offset="1"/>
    </LinearGradientBrush>










    <ScaleTransform x:Key="UniformScaleTransform" ScaleY="{Binding ScaleX, RelativeSource={RelativeSource Self}}"/>


    <!-- Motion vocabulary (HYP-199). Three durations and two easing curves cover every
         gesture in the theme; consume these instead of writing a literal.

         ⚠️ Reference them with StaticResource, never DynamicResource. WPF freezes any
         Storyboard declared inside a Style or ControlTemplate so the timing system can
         touch it off-thread, and a Freezable holding a dynamic reference cannot be
         frozen. That makes these the one group of tokens hard rule 3 does not apply to,
         and it is also why they are deliberately NOT exposed through thememodifier.yaml:
         a ThemeModifier-editable motion token could only be read via DynamicResource.

         The failure is nastier than a parse error, which is why it is worth spelling out:
         a DynamicResource here still *parses* and still *realizes* (measured), so nothing
         trips hard rule 10 and the theme loads fine. It breaks later, when the trigger
         fires and the storyboard is frozen — a dead animation rather than a red file.

         Cross-file StaticResource into this file is a shipping pattern, not a gamble:
         13 theme-only keys already do it (PlayIconGeometry, CardBackgroundBrush,
         ControlCornerRadiusMedium…), and App.xaml merges Constants.xaml first.

         Out of scope by design: looping and ambient timelines. ProgressBar's indeterminate
         sweep (RepeatBehavior="Forever", 1.7s/2s) and the Sidebar spinner's 1s rotation are
         cadences, not gestures, and Sidebar.xaml:148's 0:0:0 reset is deliberately instant. -->
    <Duration x:Key="MotionFast">0:0:0.12</Duration>
    <Duration x:Key="MotionBase">0:0:0.22</Duration>
    <Duration x:Key="MotionSlow">0:0:0.4</Duration>
    <!-- Documentation only, Nightglass (HYP-249). The dwell before the ambient backdrop commits
         a crossfade. A Style-hosted storyboard cannot reach this key (hard rule 12: a frozen
         storyboard resolves StaticResource in pre-flight, before Constants.xaml is in scope), so
         every consumer duplicates the 400ms literal locally - exactly as the durations above
         are. This key is the canonical record of intent, never referenced. -->
    <Duration x:Key="DwellDelay">0:0:0.4</Duration>
    <CubicEase x:Key="EaseStandard" EasingMode="EaseOut"/>
    <BackEase x:Key="EaseEmphasized" EasingMode="EaseOut" Amplitude="0.3"/>

    <ElasticEase x:Key="FlyOutEase" Oscillations="1"/>

